# Java style

Match the pre-2026 sources. Code added in 2026 drifted (long javadocs, same-line `if (x) return;`, column-aligned comments). Do not copy that drift.

## Keywords are not methods

Write a space after `if`, `for`, `while`, `switch`, `catch`, and `synchronized`:

```java
if (folders != null)
    this.folders = new HashMap<>(folders);

for (byte[] data : files)
{
    fimgWriter.write(data);
}
```

Older files often use `if(` and `for(`. That was a slip, not the style. Do not replicate it. `if` and `for` are keywords, not methods. Method calls, constructor calls, and declarations stay tight: `readInt()`, `new MemBuf()`, `void save()`.

The Eclipse profile inserts the keyword space and leaves call parentheses alone. Spotless applies that profile to a Java file the next time the file changes relative to `origin/main`. This repository is not being reformatted up front, so an old `if(` can still be sitting in a file you did not touch. When you do touch that file, let the formatter fix it.

## Braces

Allman braces for named types, constructors, methods, and `if` / `for` / `while` / `switch` blocks. The `{` is alone on the next line. `else`, `catch`, and `finally` each start their own line.

```java
public class Fnt
{
    public int getIdOf(String name)
    {
        if (requestedPath.length == 1)
        {
            return searchFolder.firstId;
        }
        else
        {
            return -1;
        }
    }
}
```

Anonymous classes and array initializers keep `{` on the same line: `new FileProcessingData() {`, `{0, 0, 0, 0}`.

End-of-line braces show up in a minority of files (`MemBuf`, `Buffer`, `BinaryWriter`, parts of PokEditor). Do not spread that shape.

## Control-flow bodies

A single-statement `if` or `else` may be unbraced. The statement goes on the next line, indented one level. Never `if (x) return;`.

`for` and `while` are braced even when the body is one statement. Brace new loops.

## Methods, signatures, tests

Leave one blank line between methods. The formatter preserves a blank line that is already there and will not insert one you forgot.

Keep a method signature on one line, including the long ones.

Break every chained `assertThat` onto its own indented line:

```java
assertThat(folder.getIdOf("Alpha"))
        .isEqualTo(0);
```

## Comments and binary I/O

A javadoc is one summary line, plus `@param`, `@return`, or `@throws` only when they add something the signature does not. Most methods have no javadoc. Inline notes stay short.

Structured bytes go through `MemBuf`. `Buffer` and `BinaryWriter` are the older Nds4j exceptions.

## Layout that already holds

Four spaces, no tabs. UTF-8, LF, newline at end of file. One space around operators and after commas. A cast has a space after it: `(int) value`. `Type[]`, not `Type []`. Lambdas are `x -> expr` with spaces around `->`. A ternary stays on one line.

Imports are not reordered by the tools. When you write them, group project (`io.github.turtleisaac`), then other libraries, then `java` / `javax`, then static, with a blank line between groups. Wildcards are allowed. PokEditor Swing files often lead with `javax` instead; that disagrees with Nds4j, so follow the file you are in.

Nds4j switches are `case` / `break` with an Allman brace (the sources are Java 8). PokEditor and PokEditor-Core are Java 20 and mostly use arrow switches. Do not introduce arrow switches in Nds4j.

## Tooling

`mvn verify` runs Spotless in check mode and Checkstyle. Spotless uses `config/eclipse-formatter.xml` (Eclipse JDT 4.41) and only looks at Java files that differ from `origin/main`, so existing sources are not rewritten by this setup. Checkstyle (`config/checkstyle.xml`) checks every file for LF, a trailing newline, and no tab characters.

`mvn spotless:apply` formats those changed files. It does not reorder imports, rewrap comments, or wrap long lines.

## NitroViewer

`nitroviewer-core` is its own Maven module (`com.nitroviewer.core`). Copy this rule, `config/eclipse-formatter.xml`, `config/checkstyle.xml`, and `.editorconfig` into that repo and add the same two plugins to `nitroviewer-core/pom.xml`, again with `ratchetFrom` set to `origin/main`.
